  "id": "chunk_4",
  "chunk": "they eventually recognize that repeating ineffective strategies is futile and change\ntheir course of action accordingly. This self-awareness represents cognitive abil-\nities that current AI systems seem to lack. The absence of such metacognitive\ncapabilities relates to what Rota [33] termed the “barrier of meaning” in AI: the\nfundamental challenge of how computational systems can genuinely understand\nconcepts rather than merely utilizing a “bag of tricks” that onlyseemsintelli-\ngent. The bigger the bag, the more “intelligent” AI seems to be. While LLMs\ncan generate text with remarkable fluency, they lack genuine understanding of\nthe concepts they process [3]. Without having interaction with the real-world\nlike humans do, these systems struggle to adaptively reason when familiar pat-\nterns fail or training data falls short. For example, it does not take a human\n—even an infant— an excessive amount of training data (e.g., pictures of a cat)",
